{
  "result": "On Tuesday, the city installed a bench that faced nothing in particular. The other benches had assignments: watch the river, admire the fountain, keep an eye on the children. This one looked across a narrow street at a brick wall with a drainpipe running crookedly down the middle of it.\n\nAt first, people sat there only to retie a shoe or answer a message. Then a baker began eating her lunch there. From the bench, she could see a thumb-sized gap between the drainpipe and the wall. Each day at precisely noon, a sparrow emerged from it, looking faintly annoyed.\n\nA man who walked his dog at noon started bringing an extra orange for the baker. She brought him the rolls that had split in the oven and couldn't be sold. They talked for three weeks before either thought to ask the other's name. The dog already knew them both.\n\nIn October, a municipal employee arrived with a wrench. The bench had been installed backward, he explained. It was supposed to face a small square where a maple was turning red. The baker looked over her shoulder. It was, she had to admit, an exceptionally handsome tree in the sunlight.\n\nThe man with the wrench was early for his next job, so he sat down while the baker finished her lunch. At noon the sparrow came out. It landed on his boot, considered the laces, and flew off. Without getting up, he put the wrench back in his canvas bag.",
